We have derived improved differentiability properties for solutions to a non-autonomous obstacle problem, formulated as
  \begin{equation*}
    \min \left\{ \int_{\Omega} f\left(x, \nabla u, \nabla^2 u\right) dx, \int_{\Omega} \omega(x) |\u - \obst|^2 dx \right\},
  \end{equation*}
  where the function $f$ strictly decreases with the magnitude of the gradient variable, and the problem involves a given obstacle and a strictly parabolic function. When the spatial dimension is two or three, and the growth of the function is not too rapid, we have established differentiability of solutions with respect to their second derivatives, given the differentiability of $f$ and a sufficiently smooth obstacle. This result is achieved by transforming the obstacle problem into a variational inequality and utilizing a verification argument.
